{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>Sell me as this day your birthright.</b> כְּיוֹם דִּלְהֵן — as it is clear this day, so sell me a complete sale, the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 25:31). And its plain sense is 'at this time,' as in 'And you stand as this day and I will make known to you the word of Elohim' (1 Samuel 9:27), 'You shall find him as this day' (1 Samuel 9:13), 'They will offer as this day the fat' (1 Samuel 2:16), 'And shame of face is ours as this day' (Daniel 9:7).\n\nAnd what appears from the opinion of Onkelos (Targum Onkelos on Genesis 25:31) is that since the transfer of the birthright would take effect after their father's death, he said: sell me the birthright for whatever day it will fall on, and this is the usage of 'לְהֵן' in Aramaic — לְהֵן אַתְּ אָזֵל — 'to whatever place,' מִן הֵן אַתְּ מוֹדַע לִי, and so in the portion of Vayishlaḥ (Bereshit Rabbah 78:1), וּלְהֵן אִינּוּן אָזְלִין, מִן הֵן דְּאִתְבְּרוּן, and this is a usage customary for them in many places. And in Daniel (Daniel 2:11) with a prefixed lamed, with the vocalization אֱלָהֵן, 'לָהֵן אֱלָהִין דִּי מְדָרְהוֹן עִם בִּשְׂרָא לָא אִיתוֹהִי,' and so 'לָהֵן מַלְכָּא מִלְכִּי יִשְׁפַּר עֲלָךְ' (Daniel 4:24). And Onkelos translated it otherwise than 'אֱלָהֵין' — rather, 'הֵן.' And in verified and precise manuscripts of the Targum it reads 'כְּיוֹם דִּלְהֵי,' and it is as I explained, for הֵי in their language means 'which/what,' as they say in the Talmud (Bava Kamma 99a) הֵי רַבִּי מֵאִיר, הֵי רַבִּי יְהוּדָה (Bava Batra 101a), and others like these.\n\nAnd it is possible that Onkelos rendered כַיּוֹם as though it were בַּיּוֹם — 'a sale on the day that the birthright comes into effect,' for we find this usage of the כָּף in 'כַּאֲשֶׁר יֵלֵכוּ אֶפְרוֹשׂ עֲלֵיהֶם רִשְׁתִּי' (Hosea 7:12), its meaning is בַּאֲשֶׁר, 'וְכִדְמֵי בָנַיִךְ אֲשֶׁר נָתַתְּ לָהֶם' (Ezekiel 16:36), 'כִּי כְּאַרְבַּע רוּחוֹת הַשָּׁמַיִם פֵּרַשְׂתִּי אֶתְכֶם' (Zechariah 2:10).\n\nAnd some say (Radak in the name of his father) that the price of the birthright was not the stew; rather, Scripture relates that when he wanted to eat and he was exhausted, Jacob said to him: sell me your birthright for silver, and afterward I will eat. And he answered him in his recklessness, concerning the eating: 'Why is this birthright to me? It is already sold to you,' and he swore concerning it, and they sat to eat and drink, and Scripture did not specify the price. And this is not my opinion."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "In the Daniel 4:24 citation, the verse number in the Aramaic chapters of Daniel differs from the Hebrew; Ramban cites it as 'שם ד כד' following the Aramaic/Septuagint numbering, which corresponds to Daniel 4:27 in the Hebrew Bible."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:35:32.170822065Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}